    "price_as_of": "2026-10-02T20:02:04Z (regular-session close, Yahoo chart API)",
    "balance_sheet_date": "2026-06-30",
    "share_count_date": "2026-06-30",
    "share_count_basis": "851M diluted weighted-average shares, Q2-2026 10-Q (WeightedAverageNumberOfDilutedSharesOutstanding). Held FLAT. SBC is close to zero and expensed in the flows. Buybacks were $1.0B in FY2025 and nil in H1-2026, so the count is not shrinking.",
    "wacc_rationale": "USD build. Risk-free rate ~4.3% (10y UST), beta ~1.0, ERP 5.0%, so cost of equity ~9.3-9.5%. Pre-tax cost of debt ~5.0%, ~3.8% after tax. Weights ~80/20 equity/debt at market (about $79B market cap vs ~$20B net debt), giving ~8.3%. Rounded to 8.5% and held the same in every scenario: cyclical, labour and Amazon risk are charged in the scenario flows and weights, not in the discount rate.",
    "lease_treatment": "US GAAP ASC 842. Operating-lease cost (~$954M cash in FY2025) is already an operating expense inside EBITDA, so the $4.46B of operating-lease liabilities are EXCLUDED from net debt (treatment B for operating leases). Finance leases are small (~$133M/yr principal), sit inside total_debt and are treated as debt (treatment A). Each lease class is charged once.",
    "nci_treatment": "NCI of $32M (2026-06-30) deducted in the equity bridge. The flows are consolidated. The amount is immaterial.",
    "pension_treatment": "The flows are built on EBITDA, which carries only pension SERVICE cost. Cash contributions above that (FY2025 $1,361M total contributions) fund the existing deficit. So the balance-sheet deficit (PensionAndOtherPostretirementDefinedBenefitPlansLiabilitiesNoncurrent $6,341M at 2026-06-30) goes into the equity bridge after tax at 24%, giving -$4,819M. It is charged once, as a debt-like claim, and not also inside the flows.",
    "notes": "MODEL: owner-FCF component DCF, with the cyclical adaptations (references/owner-fcf.md plus owner-fcf-adaptations.md: leases, pension, risk charged once). UPS is an integrated parcel and logistics operating company, not a bank, REIT, holdco or shell, and there is no prior memory entry. Fresh build: no prior DCF or Drivers file existed. FY0 is FY2025 (revenue $88,661M, GAAP EBITDA $11,613M = 13.1%, D&A $3,746M = 4.2%, capex $3,685M = 4.2%, OCF $8,450M, reported FCF $4,765M). Owner FCF = 4,765 - 73 SBC - 0 interest income = $4,692M. UPS has net DEBT of $19.8B, so there is no interest-income strip. Reported FCF is after ~$1.0B of interest paid. The engine builds unlevered flows and deducts net debt once, so interest is not double-counted. Net debt = total debt $24,484M (incl. current $634M and finance leases) - cash $4,653M at 2026-06-30. Marketable securities (~$68M at Sep-25, not tagged at Jun-26) are ignored as immaterial. MID-CYCLE, NOT PEAK: FY2021-22 EBITDA margins of 16.2% were the pandemic peak. The FY2016-2025 ten-year average GAAP EBITDA margin is ~14.2%. The base case matures at 14.5%, which is mid-cycle plus a partial, net-of-wage-inflation credit for the Network of the Future $3B savings target. It does NOT return to the peak. FY2026 margin is set at 12.5%, below the ~13.8% implied by guided adjusted operating profit of $8.65B plus D&A, because transformation and buyout cash costs (~$891M charge, up to 7,500 driver buyouts) are real cash this year. H1-2026 GAAP EBITDA was only 9.5% of revenue, partly non-cash impairments from facility closures. FY2026 revenue growth of 2.9% ties to the guided ~$91.2B and includes the Andlauer Healthcare acquisition (~$1.97B of acquisition cash in FY2025). Cap multiple: 13x base, 12x bear, 14x bull (cyclical range, never pandemic-peak multiples). Gordon at 8.5%/2% is ~15.7x, so the cap binds. Buybacks: $1.0B in FY2025, nil in H1-2026, and dividends of ~$5.4B/yr absorb essentially all FCF. The flat count is a modelling convention that the company is not currently funding with repurchases.",
    "dividend_note": "Dividends paid were $5,398M in FY2025 and $2,708M in H1-2026, against FY2025 reported FCF of $4,765M. FY2025 FCF did not cover the dividend, and FY2026 guidance of ~$5.5B FCF barely does. The DCF values the owner flows, not the dividend. A dividend paid above FCF is funded by debt and shows up in future net debt, not in this valuation.",
